#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "31b9e2d8-1458-50b3-a920-703476825420" :question/math-academy-id "e-3631" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y =  3\\cos x  + 4\\sin x + 2x^3.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\sin x ) = \\cos x,$ and that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} ( \\cos x ) = -\\sin x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3cos⁡x+4sin⁡x+2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+2(3){x}^{2} \\\\  & =-3sin⁡x+4cos⁡x+6{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ae922c00-2ff5-5a46-805a-62955bc5a08a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3632" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y = 4\\tan x + 5x$ at $x = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope at $x=0$ is given by the value of the derivative $y'$ at $x=0.$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} (\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4tan⁡x+5x) \\\\  & =4{sec}^{2}⁡x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =4{sec}^{2}⁡0+5 \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope is $9.$"}]}
